#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096353 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "7b49d6ec-658f-5d2a-b043-a0646ad5f904" :question/math-academy-id "e-4046" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\dfrac{7 x^2 - 2x - 13}{(x^2+1)(x+1)} = \\dfrac{9x+B}{x^2 +1} - \\dfrac{2}{x+1}$,\n\nwhat is the value of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x^2+1)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x^{2} - 2x - 13 &= (9x + B)(x + 1) - 2(x^{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7(0)^{2} - 2(0) - 13 &= (9 \\cdot (0) + B)(0 + 1) - 2((0)^{2} + 1) \\\\\n- 13 &= B - 2 \\\\\nB &= -11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096411 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "f2483ab1-2728-5a0f-b3e2-7388b5051493" :question/math-academy-id "e-504" :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$, $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x + 3)/((x^{2} + 4)(x - 1)) &= \\frac{Ax + B}{x^{2} + 4} + \\frac{C}{x - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since $x^2+4$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $(x^2+4)(x-1)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3 &= (Ax + B)(x - 1) + C(x^{2} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(1) + 3 &= (A(1) + B)(1 - 1) + C(1^{2} + 4) \\\\\n5 &= 5C \\\\\nC &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(0) + 3 &= (A(0) + B)(0 - 1) + C(0^{2} + 4) \\\\\n3 &= -B + 4C \\\\\n3 &= -B + 4(1) \\\\\nB &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A$, we choose $x=2$ and use our results for $B$ and $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(2) + 3 &= (A(2) + B)(2 - 1) + C(2^{2} + 4) \\\\\n7 &= 2A + B + 8C \\\\\n7 &= 2A + 1 + 8(1) \\\\\n2A &= -2 \\\\\nA &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=-1$, $B=1$, and $C=1$. Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{1-x}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}\n$$"}]}
